const featuredResourceSlugs = new Set(['draftsman-vs-architect-vs-engineer','what-is-included-in-permit-ready-plans','home-addition-permit-drawings','how-much-do-drafting-services-cost','remote-drafting-measurement-guide']);
const resourceDetails = {
  'draftsman-vs-architect-vs-engineer': `<h2>Choose by responsibility, not job title</h2><p>A residential drafter documents an agreed design and construction scope. An architect provides architectural services where design responsibility or licensure is required. An engineer evaluates and designs structural or other technical systems within the engineer’s discipline. One project may need more than one of these roles.</p><h2>A practical decision framework</h2><ol><li>Ask the building department whether stamped architectural or engineering documents are required.</li><li>Identify structural changes, unusual site conditions, and work outside a drafting scope.</li><li>Confirm who owns design decisions and consultant coordination.</li><li>Get each provider’s deliverables and exclusions in writing.</li></ol><p>Timpson starts by reviewing the location, project type, source documents, and intended use of the drawings. If the known scope indicates outside professional work, that dependency should be identified before the drawing package is promised.</p>`,
  'what-is-included-in-permit-ready-plans': `<h2>“Permit-ready” describes a defined package, not guaranteed approval</h2><p>A residential permit package commonly combines a cover sheet, site information, floor plans, exterior elevations, building sections, and project-specific details. The exact sheets depend on the jurisdiction, occupancy, project scope, and supporting consultants.</p><h2>Confirm these items before drafting</h2><ul><li>The authority having jurisdiction and its current checklist</li><li>Property survey, setbacks, utility, and site information</li><li>Existing-condition documentation for additions or remodels</li><li>Energy, structural, civil, geotechnical, or other consultant requirements</li><li>File format, submission method, and responsible applicant</li></ul><p>Timpson defines the proposed sheet list in the quote. Review comments may still require revisions, and permit approval remains with the reviewing authority.</p>`,
  'home-addition-permit-drawings': `<h2>Addition drawings must connect existing and proposed conditions</h2><p>A useful addition set shows what exists, what will be removed or changed, and how the new space relates to the home and property. The first risk is often incomplete existing information, not the proposed room layout.</p><h2>Information that improves the first review</h2><ul><li>Property address and jurisdiction</li><li>Survey or reliable site dimensions</li><li>Existing floor plans, photographs, and measurements</li><li>Desired rooms, approximate area, and connection points</li><li>Known structural, utility, drainage, or access constraints</li></ul><p>Timpson reviews whether remote documentation is adequate or field measurement should be considered. Structural openings, foundations, roof connections, and other engineered elements may require an outside professional.</p>`,
  'how-much-do-drafting-services-cost': `<h2>Drafting cost follows scope and uncertainty</h2><p>There is no responsible single price for every home project. A small revision to an editable plan is fundamentally different from measuring an existing home, designing an addition, or coordinating a new custom-home package.</p><h2>Use these pricing questions</h2><ul><li>Are reliable source drawings available and editable?</li><li>How much existing-condition measurement is required?</li><li>Which sheets and revisions are included?</li><li>Are site, structural, energy, or other consultants separate?</li><li>Who coordinates permit comments after the first submission?</li></ul><p>Timpson provides a project-based quote after reviewing the location, approximate size, intended work, source files, and schedule. The clearest way to control cost is to resolve missing information and decision ownership before production begins.</p>`,
  'remote-drafting-measurement-guide': `<h2>Remote drafting depends on a complete, checkable record</h2><p>Remote work can be effective when photographs, dimensions, sketches, surveys, and existing plans describe the same conditions without contradiction. A few isolated measurements are rarely enough for an addition or as-built plan.</p><h2>Measurement package checklist</h2><ul><li>Overall exterior dimensions and wall thickness where known</li><li>Room dimensions and ceiling heights</li><li>Door and window widths, heights, and locations</li><li>Stair geometry and level changes</li><li>Plumbing fixtures, equipment, and utility locations</li><li>Wide photographs plus detail photographs labeled to the sketch</li></ul><p>Measure important dimensions in more than one direction so totals can be checked. If the information does not reconcile, Timpson may request clarification, additional measurements, or local field verification before relying on it.</p>`,
};
